WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.069
Debug, factory, diagnostic, and
maintenance interfaces are useful.

2
00:00:04.149 --> 00:00:06.448
Engineers need visibility during bring-up,

3
00:00:06.528 --> 00:00:09.371
manufacturing needs test and provisioning
workflows,

4
00:00:09.451 --> 00:00:12.294
and service teams need ways to diagnose
field problems.

5
00:00:12.374 --> 00:00:16.790
The security question is which
capabilities remain available in production and under

6
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:17.535
what policy.

7
00:00:17.615 --> 00:00:21.063
Interfaces such as debug ports, service
shells, factory commands,

8
00:00:21.143 --> 00:00:23.018
test modes, maintenance protocols,

9
00:00:23.098 --> 00:00:26.002
and special boot options should have clear
access rules.

10
00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:29.772
The design should say which build profiles
enable them, what they can do,

11
00:00:29.852 --> 00:00:33.481
what state they expose, and how production
configuration is verified.

12
00:00:33.561 --> 00:00:35.921
Production builds should disable,
restrict,

13
00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:40.356
or authenticate sensitive diagnostic
behavior according to the product risk model.

14
00:00:40.436 --> 00:00:43.582
A command that reads internal state,
changes configuration,

15
00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:45.779
performs provisioning, clears storage,

16
00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:48.581
or changes operating mode deserves
explicit review.

17
00:00:48.661 --> 00:00:51.988
Leaving it enabled because it was useful
in the lab is not a policy.

18
00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:56.423
Manufacturing flows should separate test
convenience from field-device security.

19
00:00:56.503 --> 00:01:00.617
The factory may need temporary access,
calibration commands, or detailed logs.

20
00:01:00.697 --> 00:01:05.113
Field devices should not silently inherit
every manufacturing capability unless the

21
00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:07.794
product has a documented reason and a
control plan.

22
00:01:07.874 --> 00:01:11.443
Maintenance access should be documented,
logged where appropriate,

23
00:01:11.523 --> 00:01:13.458
and limited to authorized workflows.

24
00:01:13.538 --> 00:01:16.623
Support teams need clear procedures, not
hidden shortcuts.

25
00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:21.119
The device should provide useful
diagnostics while avoiding unnecessary exposure of

26
00:01:21.199 --> 00:01:23.861
secrets, private data, or broad control
functions.

27
00:01:23.941 --> 00:01:28.115
Release review should confirm that
development-only behavior does not remain in

28
00:01:28.195 --> 00:01:29.344
production firmware.

29
00:01:29.424 --> 00:01:33.175
Check build flags, service commands, debug
output, bootloader options,

30
00:01:33.255 --> 00:01:36.642
diagnostic shells, factory modes, and
provisioning interfaces.

31
00:01:36.722 --> 00:01:40.896
A repeatable checklist is often more
valuable than relying on memory at the end of a

32
00:01:40.976 --> 00:01:41.460
release.
